WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.949
I think there's a lot of existential

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.079
angst, and psychedelics have

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.269
for millennia represented

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.549
hope in transition moments.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.139
Erica Dyck is a professor at the

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.699
University of Saskatchewan who

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.829
studies the history of psychedelics

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.209
with a special interest in the

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.189
legacy of Humphry Osmond, the

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.049
British born psychiatrist who

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.969
coined the term "psychedelic,"

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.949
gave Aldous Huxley his first

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.289
dose of mescaline and conducted

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.959
pathbreaking work using LSD

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.299
to help alcoholics stop drinking.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.309
Among Osmond's best known patients

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.439
was Bill W., the co-founder

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.089
of Alcoholics Anonymous.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.739
Reason sat down with Dyck at the

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.599
Psychedelic Science 2023

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.849
Conference held in Denver this June,

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.949
where a reported 13,000

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.019
people gathered to talk about all

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.759
aspects of today's psychedelic

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.989
renaissance.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.849
We talked about why drugs such

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.159
as MDMA, psilocybin and LSD

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.629
are making a comeback.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.789
How tensions are rising between

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.379
indigenous people and medical

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.879
practitioners and whether

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.889
prohibitionists have finally

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.569
lost the war on drugs.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.909
What does this psychedelic

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.170
renaissance mean to you?

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:14.929
I am a historian who's been studying

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.969
the psychedelics for over

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.109
20 years.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.549
And so the "psychedelic renaissance"

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.019
is actually kind of a confusing term

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.029
because it's quite new, and yet

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.369
it invokes this sort of deep history

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.259
and this really deep cultural

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.699
transformation.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.589
And as a historian, I guess I'm

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:31.969
a little bit nervous about that as

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:32.959
to whether or not we've really

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.369
reached that point where we can call

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.019
this a renaissance moment.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.909
But I'm also really curious about

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.319
what it means in terms of what we're

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.149
trying to revive and

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.769
what we're trying to resurrect from

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.809
the past that might be worthwhile

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.129
in terms of sort of charting a

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.479
psychedelic future.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.099
And I think there's rich opportunity

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.449
to think about some of the different

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.369
ways that psychedelics and

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.709
psychedelic thinking has really

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.449
come to bear meaning in this

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.469
historical moment.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:59.299
Well, let's talk about the

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.639
you know, LSD was synthesized

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.650
in 1938.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:06.679
You know, started getting used about

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.809
60 years ago, right?

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.049
43, whatever.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.050
I'm an English major.

00:02:11.889 --> 00:02:11.926
I know right?

00:02:11.927 --> 00:02:12.927
But

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:15.829
then in the fifties and sixties,

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:17.239
there was this first moment where

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.069
psychedelics were being used in a

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.019
wide variety of contexts for a wide

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.459
variety of reasons.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:23.959
What are the lessons that we should

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.550
be learning from that moment?

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.189
Yeah. This kind of awakening moment

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:29.419
when the word "psychedelic" was

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.429
first coined and kind of coalesces

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.739
around a bright a variety of

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.239
medical experimentation is this

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:36.589
awakening moment, I think, for

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.869
psychedelics in the 20th century.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:39.799
And yet there were lots of

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.779
challenges in that moment prior to

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.669
the kind of countercultural uptake

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:45.829
of these substances as well.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.239
Challenges in how to measure

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.439
experience.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.089
Who is experience as matter?

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.949
Who should we draw those experiences

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.719
from? Whether it's indigenous

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.999
communities or midwives

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.769
or even spiritualists and people

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.779
working on paranormal questions

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.669
with respect to psychedelics.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:02.689
And I think we haven't really

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.189
answered that question.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:05.569
And then as psychedelics moved

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:06.949
underground, those questions lay

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.539
dormant. And there's a tendency, I

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.489
think, to kind of push against

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.079
the past and suggest that, you know,

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:13.909
now we're doing it anew or

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.289
in a fresh way.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:16.459
But I like the concept of

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.349
renaissance that also reminds us to

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.239
think about what we missed in

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.039
the past and maybe resurrecting some

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.989
of those pieces. So I think that

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:25.939
kind of wild landscape

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.129
for experimentation of the 1950s

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.019
is something we should embrace here

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:30.619
again.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.719
Can you talk about Humphry

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.699
Osmond, who's the guy who coined

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:35.930
the term psychedelic,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.739
you know, skipped around the globe,

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.429
ended up in Canada, where he was

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:41.899
doing a lot of this work.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.819
What is his contribution

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.709
or what is his role to

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:47.629
the psychedelic, I guess,

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:48.769
moment in the fifties?

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.119
But then what should we be learning

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.120
from him now?

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:53.749
Humphry Osmond is such a fascinating

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.119
character, you know, not only for

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.219
deeply investing in the word itself

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.749
"psychedelic," which is, you know,

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.579
"to bring light to the psyche"

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.469
or Delos, "to bring to light"

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.329
or introduce it in 1956

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.309
by saying "to fathom hell or

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:08.869
angelic, you'll need a pinch of

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.969
psychedelic." The first uttering

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:11.959
of it.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.819
It's like an advertising jingle.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.319
It is a bit. And he played around

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:16.969
with this concept and you can find

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.528
different rhyming couplets online.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:20.359
And Aldous Huxley, his

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.129
friend, came up with his own sort of

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:23.179
matches.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.889
But, you know, I think when I

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.719
interviewed Humphry Osmond in the

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.669
early 2000s,

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:29.899
the conversation around psychedelics

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.069
was quite different at that time.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.629
And, you know, he wasn't getting his

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.669
phone wasn't ringing off the hook.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.799
But, you know, I asked him what

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.049
his sort of proudest

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.729
moment was or what he thought his

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:42.199
legacy might be.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:43.129
And these are always awkward

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:44.269
questions for people, I think, at

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:46.249
the best of times. But what

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:47.809
he told me was surprising.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:50.059
He said that he was really proud

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.369
of helping to found Schizophrenics

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:53.389
Anonymous.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:54.259
Hmm.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.029
And I've been reflecting on that for

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.469
some time and wondered whether this

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:58.549
was because he didn't want to talk

00:04:58.550 --> 00:04:59.839
about psychedelics in that moment

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.609
because they were considered taboo.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:02.989
But I actually think there's more to

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:03.990
it.

